WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:03.469
Film Courage: A lot of our viewers ask about
pitch meetings and how to acquire one.

00:00:03.469 --> 00:00:05.470
How does someone get a pitch meeting at your
company?

00:00:05.470 --> 00:00:14.269
Scott Kirkpatrick: Well DRG is a bit different
where we’re mostly co-producing with major

00:00:14.269 --> 00:00:17.080
networks or major production companies.

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:20.360
So there is already a kind of established
relationship there.

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:25.470
And I guess that’s sort of the key point
in terms of how do you begin that process.

00:00:25.470 --> 00:00:31.950
How does one open the door to get that kind
of open communication with an acquisitions

00:00:31.950 --> 00:00:32.950
team or development team.

00:00:32.950 --> 00:00:42.010
So not just at DRG but any company you kind
of have to understand what that company wants,

00:00:42.010 --> 00:00:49.570
what they work with, what they like, what
genres of content they like to work with,

00:00:49.570 --> 00:00:52.630
getting a sense of who their key clients.

00:00:52.630 --> 00:01:04.110
For instance at DRG we work a great deal at
Netflix, RLJ Acorn where there are specific

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:05.979
networks that we have great relationships
with.

00:01:05.979 --> 00:01:12.250
When I was at MarVista Entertainment, we had
great relationships with Lifetime (LMN), Disney,

00:01:12.250 --> 00:01:13.250
Nickelodeon.

00:01:13.250 --> 00:01:19.180
So at MarVista we were producing content that
was kind of catered toward those audiences

00:01:19.180 --> 00:01:24.370
(Ion Networks and a few others) and a few
others and at DRG we have a different set

00:01:24.370 --> 00:01:26.460
of companies that we work closely with.

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:29.490
That we just have a library of media that
works well with those.

00:01:29.490 --> 00:01:35.340
So our acquisitions team has a different mandate
than what the acquisitions teams at MarVista

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:36.340
had.

00:01:36.340 --> 00:01:41.790
So if you’re going to go into a company
and attempt to build that kind of that initial

00:01:41.790 --> 00:01:47.030
‘let’s see if some of my ideas are going
to work well and we can get something going.’

00:01:47.030 --> 00:01:52.790
You have to first understand what the company
does, what it is they like to work with, what

00:01:52.790 --> 00:01:58.700
media they’re actually in need of, and then
the best way to get a pitch meeting is to

00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:06.630
poise your kind of introduction as a way of
being able to serve their needs to help them

00:02:06.630 --> 00:02:08.770
accomplish their content goals.

00:02:08.770 --> 00:02:19.210
So if they’re looking for women-in-peril
films, if they’re looking for action films,

00:02:19.210 --> 00:02:22.709
going into those companies, pitching those
two scripts are going to be great.

00:02:22.709 --> 00:02:27.640
If you go in pitching stuff that has nothing
to do with their core values of the company

00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:31.530
in terms of what they’re identified with
in the marketplace, you’re not going to

00:02:31.530 --> 00:02:32.990
get a warm reception.

00:02:32.990 --> 00:02:39.099
It’s just they’re looking for ways to
satisfy finding the content that they need.

00:02:39.099 --> 00:02:43.319
So if you can position yourself where you’re
kind of answering to that need, it’s the

00:02:43.319 --> 00:02:44.319
best way to get it.

00:02:44.319 --> 00:02:46.330
Film Courage: I know with MarVista [and your
2015 interview series with us there] we talked

00:02:46.330 --> 00:02:51.230
about the ‘Goldmine Genre’ and it was
Women-in-Peril or Tween Comedies or things

00:02:51.230 --> 00:02:53.750
like that (films that were lighter in content).

00:02:53.750 --> 00:02:59.629
It sounds like with DRG some of the darker
topics/films such as the Norwegian noir is

00:02:59.629 --> 00:03:00.750
more acceptable.

00:03:00.750 --> 00:03:07.110
So it’s finding out what a company a writer
would potentially like to pitch to and what

00:03:07.110 --> 00:03:08.110
they represent.

00:03:08.110 --> 00:03:10.640
So maybe a Tween Comedy wouldn’t work for
DRG?

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:13.170
Scott Kirkpatrick: For DRG a teen comedy wouldn’t
work at all.

00:03:13.170 --> 00:03:18.190
Two years ago when I was at MarVista I would
have jumped up and down if you gave me an

00:03:18.190 --> 00:03:19.190
opportunity.

00:03:19.190 --> 00:03:20.680
It’s because the mandate of the company
is different.

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:22.320
There’s just a different need.

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:29.410
And in addition to dark, Nordic noirs and
other deep serious scripted content we also

00:03:29.410 --> 00:03:32.440
have a great deal of one-off factual films.

00:03:32.440 --> 00:03:37.360
This is stuff that at MarVista would never
work but we have a massive library of one-hour

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:43.440
documentary type programs that do amazing
in the digital space.

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:46.110
We have a great collection of game show formats.

00:03:46.110 --> 00:03:52.390
We have a great collection of what we call
lifestyle programming where it’s cooking

00:03:52.390 --> 00:03:55.530
and DIY type stuff.

00:03:55.530 --> 00:03:57.970
It’s a totally different library.

00:03:57.970 --> 00:04:02.610
Every company has a completely different package
that they’re selling to the marketplace.

00:04:02.610 --> 00:04:07.409
Distribution companies to be successful, they
need to be kind of branded as one thing or

00:04:07.409 --> 00:04:10.319
another.

00:04:10.319 --> 00:04:14.680
Because they are branded that way, it attracts
certain types of buyers in the international

00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:15.680
marketplace.

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:18.339
There are only so many channels in the world,
there are only so many media buyers in the

00:04:18.339 --> 00:04:20.469
world, and they all have limited budgets.

00:04:20.469 --> 00:04:25.909
So distribution companies have to position
themselves to be different in the marketplace

00:04:25.909 --> 00:04:32.050
and so they develop a kind of streamlined
system of this content works really well because

00:04:32.050 --> 00:04:33.949
we’re able to transact on it easily.

00:04:33.949 --> 00:04:35.180
Let’s get more of that.

00:04:35.180 --> 00:04:37.159
That’s kind of how they think.

00:04:37.159 --> 00:04:41.969
So to go in (not just a pitch meeting) but
if you want to showcase your script, if you

00:04:41.969 --> 00:04:49.240
want to be a producer and produce content
and get hired by a company to produce content

00:04:49.240 --> 00:04:53.990
for them, you have to be able to position
yourself to that company to say “I already

00:04:53.990 --> 00:04:56.669
do stuff like that, therefore I can help you
out.”

00:04:56.669 --> 00:04:57.669
You know what I mean?

00:04:57.669 --> 00:04:58.669
Film Courage: Right.

00:04:58.669 --> 00:05:02.879
So almost back engineer it where a filmmaker
knows that their film fits into this category

00:05:02.879 --> 00:05:05.690
and then see who has distribution that is
similar to their film.

00:05:05.690 --> 00:05:06.690
Scott Kirkpatrick: Exactly.

00:05:06.690 --> 00:05:10.020
Film Courage: But then can they just call
up…you said that there are already established

00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:11.020
relationships.

00:05:11.020 --> 00:05:12.020
I’m just curious.

00:05:12.020 --> 00:05:17.690
It’s interesting if I was a filmmaker and
had (let’s say) a noir thriller that I wanted

00:05:17.690 --> 00:05:23.139
to have considered, it sounds like that’s
not the channel or the proper way to go about

00:05:23.139 --> 00:05:24.419
it in terms of this.

00:05:24.419 --> 00:05:26.379
Scott Kirkpatrick: Reverse engineering is
exactly the way to do it.

00:05:26.379 --> 00:05:30.439
It’s just if you already have a script,
if you already have a film, or if you just

00:05:30.439 --> 00:05:34.770
have an idea or a treatment or anything along
those lines that you’re trying to get out

00:05:34.770 --> 00:05:35.770
into the marketplace.

00:05:35.770 --> 00:05:41.361
First off, if you’re only at the treatment
stage I would try to package it or get some

00:05:41.361 --> 00:05:42.740
other elements in there.

00:05:42.740 --> 00:05:48.580
But going to a distributor that is in alignment
with what other project you’re trying to

00:05:48.580 --> 00:05:55.439
pitch, that is how you begin that introduction
of this is how we can start process of being

00:05:55.439 --> 00:05:59.089
in regular conversation about this topic and
potentially future ones.

00:05:59.089 --> 00:06:03.219
It’s walking in the door by reverse engineering
it in the sense of “I know this company

00:06:03.219 --> 00:06:06.029
works in these two genres or three genres.

00:06:06.029 --> 00:06:08.169
My project fits that very, very well.”

00:06:08.169 --> 00:06:11.860
Focusing on the companies that fit that and
then talking to them basically saying “Look,

00:06:11.860 --> 00:06:16.270
I know you produced this stuff or I know you
develop or co-produce this kind of material.

00:06:16.270 --> 00:06:19.910
I have something very much in alignment with
it.

00:06:19.910 --> 00:06:20.910
Maybe we can talk about it?”

00:06:20.910 --> 00:06:27.090
And it it’s at an early stage like a treatment
or you know an initial rough draft screenplay

00:06:27.090 --> 00:06:32.659
type thing, going to them early and saying
I want to cater this for you, give me your

00:06:32.659 --> 00:06:34.029
advice, give me your feedback.

00:06:34.029 --> 00:06:39.819
Or even better if you’re a producer “Hey,
I want to produce this woman-in-peril thriller.

00:06:39.819 --> 00:06:41.699
I want to produce this tween comedy.”

00:06:41.699 --> 00:06:45.330
Going to a company that works in that and
saying “I’m going to produce it.

00:06:45.330 --> 00:06:46.330
I’m going to fund it.

00:06:46.330 --> 00:06:48.219
I’m going to figure out how to get the money
for it.

00:06:48.219 --> 00:06:53.819
What I want from you is a clear understanding
of what works.

00:06:53.819 --> 00:06:58.499
What is going to make is a great film in the
marketplace or a great product in the marketplace

00:06:58.499 --> 00:07:04.770
(and if in exchange for that) I’d love for
you to have the first look at acquiring it

00:07:04.770 --> 00:07:06.449
later.”

00:07:06.449 --> 00:07:10.409
Or even trying to sign a deal where they get
the opportunity to have it regardless and

00:07:10.409 --> 00:07:14.129
then they’re now…they have something at
the back end.

00:07:14.129 --> 00:07:17.949
They have a filmmaker that is going to produce
the whole thing and they get to coach and

00:07:17.949 --> 00:07:22.250
create the whole movie that they want and
they’re guaranteed to have it at the end

00:07:22.250 --> 00:07:23.469
of the day.

00:07:23.469 --> 00:07:24.949
That’s kind of how you do it.

00:07:24.949 --> 00:07:29.679
You create opportunities for the production
companies or for distribution companies that

00:07:29.679 --> 00:07:31.169
make their job easier.

00:07:31.169 --> 00:07:35.729
Film Courage: Is someone just calling up and
finding the right contact?

00:07:35.729 --> 00:07:42.629
I’m sure it’s a delicate process into
getting that introduction because the filmmaker

00:07:42.629 --> 00:07:47.229
doesn't want to come across too pushy, doesn’t
want to send an email with too much information,

00:07:47.229 --> 00:07:53.569
what’s the proper way to just ease in (just
a little bit) not with a lot of expectation.

00:07:53.569 --> 00:07:56.409
Not with too much pressure on the other person.

00:07:56.409 --> 00:08:03.879
Scott Kirkpatrick: I find LinkedIn is a great
source because everyone in the professional

00:08:03.879 --> 00:08:05.399
world is on it.

00:08:05.399 --> 00:08:10.400
And you look for terms like acquisitions.

00:08:10.400 --> 00:08:16.169
Anyone in the acquisitions department of a
distribution company or executive producers

00:08:16.169 --> 00:08:21.849
who work for production companies, those are
sort of the individuals tasked with making

00:08:21.849 --> 00:08:25.649
things happen, bringing stuff in.

00:08:25.649 --> 00:08:34.849
And if you’re going to be jumping out to
people blindly, they get a lot of people hounding

00:08:34.849 --> 00:08:36.560
them all the time.

00:08:36.560 --> 00:08:39.440
So that’s kind of why I go back to.

00:08:39.440 --> 00:08:44.550
You can’t look at it as what can you do
for me and how can you help me make my movie,

00:08:44.550 --> 00:08:49.360
you have to say “I understand your company
is looking for material like this and I would

00:08:49.360 --> 00:08:52.220
like to help you with your search.”

00:08:52.220 --> 00:08:57.840
If you back engineer it in that sense, you
are making their job easier.

00:08:57.840 --> 00:09:02.410
You are helping them achieve a goal that is
going to help them professionally.

00:09:02.410 --> 00:09:06.060
And now all of the sudden they are going to
be a lot more receptive to listening to you

00:09:06.060 --> 00:09:10.199
and you position yourself differently than
all the other noise that is out there.

00:09:10.199 --> 00:09:11.350
That is kind of how you do it.

00:09:11.350 --> 00:09:18.870
And from that, don’t make the mistake of
going after the top dog at every company.

00:09:18.870 --> 00:09:24.600
Like don’t go after the CEO or the head
of this or the executive VP of that because

00:09:24.600 --> 00:09:29.050
they are bombarded with work all the time
that’s just within the company, that’s

00:09:29.050 --> 00:09:32.879
not even random people reaching out.

00:09:32.879 --> 00:09:39.399
So go for juniors, go for middle managers,
go for people in companies who are hungry,

00:09:39.399 --> 00:09:43.970
who have more open time and who are looking
for those opportunities to bring stuff in

00:09:43.970 --> 00:09:48.100
because it helps them also professionally
achieve that next level in the company.

